            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, there were only a few areas associated with air leakage from your home:



** The 5" chimney for the current oil furnace &amp; the 5" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace



** Around the interior trim of the front door that has not yet been completed.



** The attic access panel can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, and you upgrade the furnace to a 95% efficient, direct-vent propane model (eliminating the need for the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipes), I estimate your house will likely achieve about a10% reduction in air leakage. As your house is considered to be a bit tight already, making it tighter means that additional ventilation will be required ....... which actually adds a heating load. As we talked in your house ............ Build Tight &amp; Ventilate Right !!



There is an estimated additional increase of about $ 209 per year in energy costs, however please keep in mind the estimated annual savings of the new furnace !! As an unexpected bonus, you would still be able to qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) for the minimum 10% reduction in air leakage, if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and there is an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of exterior insulation was added during a previous renovation. We discussed your interest to possibly add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For this house, I have modeled another R15, bringing the total R-value up to slightly more than the minimum R30 that is generally required for new construction in the North Slave district of the NWT.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 357 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), This rebate is for increasing 100% of the exterior walls, including the exterior walls of the rear entrance room (as that room is also heated space).



I also modeled 2 other levels of additional insulation:

1. Add R10 could potentially save $ 285 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



2. Add R20 could potentially save $ 411 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a kitchen range hood fan

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer

        - the above are mechanical "exhaust" appliances, and the furnace has a "passive" 5" fresh air

          air supply connected to the return air plenum to bring in some fresh air whenever the furnace

          or one of the exhaust appliances is being operated.     



Even taking the above ventilation into account, NRCan's EnerGuide Rating System software would still indicate that your house would be considered "under ventilated" if built today .......... and would require a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) be installed.



Although not a requirement for you to add an HRV today, it is one way to achieve balanced ventilation with "heat recovery" from the outgoing air (would suggest 75% efficiency), rather than just operating your current exhaust fans (with no heat recovery) more often.



The simplest solution is to just update the bathroom fan to a new Energy Star unit, and include a timer switch or even a humidistat switch. Some new ESTAR bathroom exhaust fans have the humidistat built right into the fan !! 

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 1,001 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an oil furnace with a rated 82.8% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) by NRCan of 82.8%. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more wall insulation) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,375 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 35.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to participate in the DHERP, you would still be eligible for a $1,500 rebate on this furnace through our Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Eliminating the current oil furnace also eliminates the 5" chimney &amp; the 5" combustion air supply pipe !



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



For more information and rebate application forms for EEIP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a kitchen range hood fan

        - one bathroom fan that appears to have a weak operation

        - there is a clothes dryer that also did not create much negative pressure

        - the above are mechanical "exhaust" appliances, and the furnace has a "passive" 5" fresh air

          air supply connected to the return air plenum to bring in some fresh air whenever the furnace

          or one of the exhaust appliances is being operated.     



Taking the above ventilation into account, the software would still indicate that your house would be considered "under ventilated" if built today .......... and would require a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) be installed. There is a section of my report that talks about this.



You should check the dryer vent to ensure the vent pipe is solidly connected, has no "kinks" and the vent is not plugged.



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not estimated to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vents up a chimney and can be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



